From e92cf8f1f71e557ae606736af8688f8d6843dbd1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 19:01:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] container-safe browser launch, a launch doctor, and six more
 e2e assertions
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Launch
- The Codespace run got past "executable doesn't exist" and into "Target page,
  context or browser has been closed" — Chromium started and died. That is the
  standard container failure: Chrome's setuid sandbox cannot initialise as root
  in an unprivileged container, and the default /dev/shm is 64 MB, which the
  renderer exhausts on startup. The suite now passes --no-sandbox,
  --disable-setuid-sandbox, --disable-dev-shm-usage and --disable-gpu, and
  falls back to channel:"chromium" if the headless shell will not start.
- Playwright 1.60's launch() defaults to the headless *shell*, a different
  binary from the Chrome it also downloads. Either can be the one whose
  libraries are missing, which is why the fallback exists.
- The error message now distinguishes "never downloaded" from "started and
  died" and points at `--with-deps`, which is the part a plain
  `playwright install` skips.

scripts/e2e-doctor.mjs (pnpm e2e:doctor)
- Reports node, uid, /dev/shm size, both browser paths, whether they exist,
  any `not found` shared libraries, and the result of four launch
  configurations — then names the one that worked, or the exact apt-get line
  if none did. "Target page, context or browser has been closed" is the same
  message for half a dozen causes; measuring once beats guessing across a
  round trip.

Six more assertions, 14 -> 20
- the wizard opens exactly the reviews the checklist implies: step 2 reports
  "2 von 14" and step 3 independently reports "NICHT ERFORDERLICH (12)"
- a submitted or drafted project persists and is findable in the table
- a review-status edit reaches the audit trail
- the projects page and the dashboard show the *same* total — the assertion
  the fabricated 0.86 / 0.68 multipliers would have failed
- the theme survives a reload
- CSV export produces a file whose header carries Projektnummer

One of these caught my own bad assertion rather than a defect: I first asserted
which two departments were open by slicing the page by column order, which is
layout-dependent. The count is the real invariant — 12 of 14 closed means
exactly the two answered are open, and shared/checklist.test.ts already pins
the per-department trigger rule.

Also: chunkSizeWarningLimit 900 -> 1400, since react-pdf is deliberately lazy
and warning about it every build teaches people to ignore the warning.

Gates: 0 type errors, 124 passed / 6 skipped, 85 lint warnings, verify-data
clean, build clean, e2e 20 passed / 0 failed.

